  { month: "May", tempHi: "19", tempLo: "3", rain: "30", rating: "Best" },
  { month: "Jun", tempHi: "21", tempLo: "7", rain: "80", rating: "Best" },
  { month: "Jul", tempHi: "22", tempLo: "9", rain: "150", rating: "Wet" },
  { month: "Aug", tempHi: "21", tempLo: "8", rain: "140", rating: "Wet" },
  { month: "Sep", tempHi: "19", tempLo: "5", rain: "60", rating: "Best" },
  { month: "Oct", tempHi: "15", tempLo: "0", rain: "20", rating: "Best" },
  { month: "Nov", tempHi: "10", tempLo: "-6", rain: "5", rating: "Good" },
  { month: "Dec", tempHi: "7", tempLo: "-9", rain: "2", rating: "Fair" },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: "Is Shangri-La worth visiting?", a: "Yes, absolutely. It is one of the most unique destinations in China. The combination of Tibetan Buddhist culture, dramatic Himalayan scenery, and a palpable sense of remoteness creates an experience that no other place in Yunnan offers. The altitude and journey add to the mystique." },
  { q: "Do I need a special permit to visit Shangri-La?", a: "No. Unlike the Tibet Autonomous Region, Shangri-La (Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture) does not require a special travel permit or a Tibet Travel Permit. A standard Chinese visa is sufficient for foreign visitors. You can travel independently." },
  { q: "How do I deal with the altitude sickness?", a: "Most visitors adapt within 24-48 hours. Key tips: spend your first day at a relaxed pace, drink 3-4 liters of water, avoid alcohol, eat light meals, and consider Diamox (acetazolamide) starting 1-2 days before arrival. Oxygen canisters are sold everywhere if needed." },
  { q: "When is the best time to visit Shangri-La?", a: "May to June offers blooming rhododendrons and comfortable temperatures. September to October brings clear skies, golden autumn colors, and stable weather. Avoid July-August for monsoon rains and December-February for extreme cold and possible road closures." },
  { q: "How do I get to Shangri-La from Lijiang?", a: "The Lijiang-Shangri-La Expressway takes approximately 3.5 hours by car. Daily flights from Lijiang to Diqing Shangri-La Airport (DIG) take 1 hour. Buses depart regularly from Lijiang Bus Station (4-5 hours). A high-speed railway line is under construction." },
  { q: "Is Shangri-La safe for solo travelers?", a: "Yes, Shangri-La is very safe. Violent crime is extremely rare. The main risks are altitude-related health issues and traffic safety on mountain roads. Solo female travelers report feeling comfortable here. Basic Mandarin or a translation app is helpful as English is not widely spoken." },
  { q: "What should I pack for Shangri-La?", a: "Pack layers: the temperature can swing 15 C in a day. Essentials: sunscreen (UV is intense at altitude), lip balm, moisturizer, sunglasses, comfortable walking shoes, a warm jacket even in summer, rain gear (May-Oct), and altitude sickness medication." },
  { q: "Can I use credit cards in Shangri-La?", a: "Cash is still king, especially in smaller establishments. Major hotels and upscale restaurants accept international cards, but bring RMB cash for local restaurants, taxis, and market purchases. WeChat Pay and Alipay are ubiquitous for those with Chinese bank accounts." },
  { q: "How much does a trip to Shangri-La cost?", a: "Budget travelers can manage on 200-400 RMB per day (hostel, local food, public transport). Mid-range travelers should budget 600-1,000 RMB per day. Luxury travelers can expect 1,500+ RMB per day. Flights to/from Shangri-La are additional." },
  { q: "Is English widely spoken in Shangri-La?", a: "No. English is rare outside international hotels and some upscale restaurants. Hotel front desks in Dukezong Old Town usually have someone with basic English. For everything else, download Google Translate with the Chinese offline pack or use Pleco. Learn three phrases: 'Ni hao' (hello), 'Xie xie' (thank you), and showing your hotel address card to taxi drivers." },
  { q: "Do I need a VPN to visit Shangri-La?", a: "Yes. Google, Gmail, Instagram, Facebook, WhatsApp, and Google Maps are blocked in China. Install and test a VPN before you leave home — you cannot download VPN apps after arrival. LetsVPN, Astrill, and Mullvad are reliable choices. Hotel WiFi and local SIM cards both go through the firewall." },
  { q: "Can I visit Shangri-La with China's visa-free policy?", a: "Probably yes. As of 2025-2026, 38 countries have 15-day visa-free entry (most EU, Australia, NZ, Malaysia, Japan, South Korea, etc.). An additional 54 countries qualify for 144-hour transit visa-free via Kunming, Chengdu, or Chongqing. Check China's latest visa policy on your government's travel advisory site before booking." },
  { q: "Is Shangri-La suitable for children or older travelers?", a: "Yes, with precautions. Children over 6 generally adapt well to 3,160 m. Seniors should consult a doctor about altitude and pre-existing conditions. Choose gentler activities (Old Town, Napa Lake by car, Songzanlin Monastery) and avoid Tiger Leaping Gorge trekking. Hotels in the new town offer more consistent comfort for families and older guests." },
  { q: "What is the difference between Shangri-La City and Shangri-La County?", a: "Shangri-La City (formerly Zhongdian, renamed in 2001) is the urban center and county seat of Shangri-La County within Diqing Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture. When travelers say 'Shangri-La,' they usually mean the city and its surrounding attractions within a 100 km radius. The county covers 11,613 km² — larger than Jamaica." },
  { q: "Can I hike independently or do I need a guide?", a: "Most trails are accessible independently. Tiger Leaping Gorge high trail is well-marked and dotted with guesthouses — no guide needed. Pudacuo requires you to use park shuttle buses. For remote areas like Yubeng Village or Balagezong, a local guide improves safety and experience. No special hiking permits are required for Shangri-La." },
];

// ==== NEW DATA: Shangri-La vs Tibet ====
const vsTibet = [
  { aspect: "Travel permit", shangriLa: "No permit needed. Standard Chinese visa only.", tibet: "Tibet Travel Permit + group tour + guide required.", winner: "shangri-la" },
  { aspect: "Altitude", shangriLa: "3,160 m (city). Most attractions 3,200-3,500 m.", tibet: "3,656 m (Lhasa). Everest Base Camp 5,200 m.", winner: "shangri-la" },
  { aspect: "Tibetan culture", shangriLa: "Authentic — monasteries, festivals, Tibetan majority prefecture.", tibet: "Deeper immersion but heavily regulated access.", winner: "tibet" },
  { aspect: "Cost (7 days)", shangriLa: "2,000-5,000 RMB independent travel.", tibet: "6,000+ RMB (mandatory tour package).", winner: "shangri-la" },
  { aspect: "Flexibility", shangriLa: "Independent travel, rent a car, go anywhere.", tibet: "Fixed itinerary, guide escorts, checkpoints.", winner: "shangri-la" },
  { aspect: "Landscapes", shangriLa: "Alpine lakes, gorges, snow mountains, grasslands.", tibet: "Vaster, higher, more iconic. Himalayan spine.", winner: "tibet" },
  { aspect: "Accessibility", shangriLa: "3.5 hrs from Lijiang by expressway. Direct flights.", tibet: "Train or flight. Permit processing takes 1-2 weeks.", winner: "shangri-la" },
  { aspect: "Best for", shangriLa: "First-time China travelers, independent explorers, photographers.", tibet: "Hardcore trekkers, spiritual seekers, bucket-list travelers.", winner: "depends" },
];

// ==== NEW DATA: Altitude Acclimatization Timeline ====
const acclimatizationTimeline = [
  { day: "Day 1", title: "Arrive & Rest", desc: "Check into your hotel. Walk slowly, drink 3-4L water, eat light meals. Explore Dukezong Old Town at a relaxed pace. No alcohol. Take a short evening walk to the prayer wheel." },
  { day: "Day 2", title: "Gentle Exploration", desc: "Visit Songzanlin Monastery (morning chanting is unforgettable but involves stairs — take it slow). Afternoon: Napa Lake by car. You should be 70-80% acclimatized by evening." },
  { day: "Day 3", title: "Full Activity", desc: "You're ready for Pudacuo National Park (more walking at 3,500 m). Still carry water and an oxygen canister just in case. Most people feel completely normal by now." },
  { day: "Day 4+", title: "Adventure Ready", desc: "Tiger Leaping Gorge trek, Meili Snow Mountain road trip, or Balagezong Canyon. Your body has adapted. Continue hydrating and avoid alcohol binges." },
];

// ==== NEW DATA: China Travel Starter Kit ====
const chinaEntryKit = [
  { title: "Visa Check", desc: "38 countries now have 15-day visa-free entry to China (2024-2025 policy, includes most EU, Australia, NZ, Malaysia, etc.). 54 countries qualify for 144-hour transit visa-free via Kunming/Chengdu. Check your eligibility before booking." },
  { title: "VPN Setup", desc: "Install a VPN BEFORE arriving in China. Google, Gmail, Instagram, WhatsApp, Google Maps are all blocked. Popular choices: LetsVPN, Astrill, Mullvad. Download and test at home — you cannot install after arrival." },
  { title: "Essential Apps", desc: "Install before departure: Didi (ride-hailing, works in English), Baidu Maps or Apple Maps (Google Maps is inaccurate in China), Pleco (offline Chinese dictionary), Google Translate (download Chinese offline pack)." },
  { title: "SIM Card & Internet", desc: "Buy a China Unicom or China Mobile SIM at Kunming Airport (100-200 RMB for 20GB). eSIM options: Nomad, Airalo, Holafly. Hotel WiFi is reliable but behind the firewall. Pro tip: screenshot your hotel address in Chinese for taxi drivers." },
  { title: "Payment Setup", desc: "Cash is accepted but digital payment dominates. If you can set up Alipay TourPass (links to international cards) or WeChat Pay Tour Card before arrival, you'll have a much smoother experience. Otherwise, carry enough RMB cash — ATMs work but some reject foreign cards." },
  { title: "Travel Insurance", desc: "Essential for high-altitude travel. Ensure your policy covers trekking above 3,000 m and helicopter evacuation. World Nomads, SafetyWing, and True Traveller all cover China with altitude add-ons. Hospitals in Shangri-La are basic — serious cases go to Kunming." },
];

// ==== NEW DATA: Solo Travel ====
const soloTravelTips = [
  { title: "Meeting Other Travelers", desc: "Stay at a hostel in Dukezong Old Town (Jianshe Tang, Blue Moon Valley). These attract independent travelers from around the world. Common areas and rooftop cafes are natural social hubs. Join group day trips to Tiger Leaping Gorge to find trekking partners." },
  { title: "Dining Alone", desc: "Tibetan tea houses are welcoming to solo diners. Try the momo (dumpling) shops near Sifang Street — counter seating is common. Hotpot restaurants are better with 2+ people, but most noodle shops and stir-fry places are solo-friendly." },
  { title: "Getting Around Solo", desc: "Didi works for in-town trips (8-15 RMB). For day trips, ask your hostel to pair you with other guests to share a driver (400-600 RMB split 3-4 ways). The Lijiang-Shangri-La express bus is safe and costs 80 RMB." },
  { title: "Safety Reality", desc: "Shangri-La has virtually no violent crime. The actual risks are: altitude sickness (know the symptoms), mountain road accidents (avoid night driving), and getting lost without cell signal (download offline maps). Female solo travelers consistently report feeling safe here." },
];

