      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== YUNNAN SNUB-NOSED MONKEY ===== -->
    <section id="wildlife" class="bg-white py-20 sm:py-28">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2 items-center">
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] uppercase text-emerald-600">Wildlife</p>
            <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">Yunnan Snub-Nosed Monkey</h2>
            <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">One of the world's rarest primates lives just a few hours from Shangri-La. The Yunnan snub-nosed monkey (<em>Rhinopithecus bieti</em>) is found nowhere else on Earth — only in the high-altitude forests of northwest Yunnan and southeast Tibet, between 3,000 and 4,500 meters.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">With fewer than <strong>3,000 individuals</strong> remaining in the wild, these monkeys are critically endangered. Their distinctive upturned nose, pink lips, and thick golden fur make them one of the most photogenic primates in the world. Seeing them in their natural habitat is a wildlife experience that rivals Africa's mountain gorillas.</p>
            <div class="mt-5 space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-slate-600"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold mt-0.5">→</span> <span><strong>Where:</strong> Tacheng Snub-Nosed Monkey National Park (塔城滇金丝猴国家公园), about 3 hours drive south of Shangri-La.</span></div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-slate-600"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold mt-0.5">→</span> <span><strong>Best time:</strong> Early morning (monkeys descend from the mountain around 8-9 AM for feeding). May-October offers the easiest access; winter snow can make roads difficult.</span></div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-slate-600"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold mt-0.5">→</span> <span><strong>Ticket:</strong> 100 RMB. Park rangers lead small groups to observation points about a 15-minute walk from the entrance.</span></div>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-slate-600"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold mt-0.5">→</span> <span><strong>Tip:</strong> Combine with a visit to the Lisu minority villages in the same valley. The area around Tacheng is a beautiful off-the-beaten-path detour from the Shangri-La-Lijiang route.</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-slate-200">
            <img src="/images/snub-nosed-monkey.png" alt="Yunnan snub-nosed monkey Rhinopithecus bieti in the wild at Tacheng National Park near Shangri-La" class="w-full h-full object-cover aspect-[4/3]" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== MAP ===== -->
    <section id="map" class="relative bg-white py-20 sm:py-28 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-[radial-gradient(circle_at_30%_70%,rgba(21,128,61,0.05),transparent_60%)]"></div>
      <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] uppercase text-emerald-600">Interactive</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">Map</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-lg text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-500">All landmarks on one map. Hover for names, click for details.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="map-container" class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white z-10 h-[500px] sm:h-[600px]">
          <div id="leaflet-map" class="w-full h-full"></div>
        </div>
