    <!-- ===== BEYOND THE MAIN ATTRACTIONS ===== -->
    <section id="hidden-gems" class="bg-white py-20 sm:py-28">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="mb-14">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] uppercase text-emerald-600">Hidden Gems</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">Beyond the Main Attractions</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-500">Three more places that most guidebooks miss — a monastery rivaling Songzanlin, a one-of-a-kind pagoda, and the beating heart of Dukezong's nightlife.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-amber-50 text-amber-600 text-lg font-bold">寺</span>
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Dongzhulin Monastery</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">Built in 1667, Dongzhulin (东竹林寺) is one of the 13 great monasteries of the Gelug school of Tibetan Buddhism. Located in Benzilan town, about 100 km from Shangri-La on the road to Meili Snow Mountain, it houses priceless thangkas, sculptures, and a three-story golden Buddha statue. Unlike Songzanlin, Dongzhulin sees very few tourists — the chanting hall still feels like a living monastery, not a museum.</p>
            <div class="mt-3 flex items-center justify-between rounded-lg bg-white px-3 py-2">
              <span class="text-xs text-slate-400">Entry</span>
              <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">Free / donation</span>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Tip:</strong> Stop here en route to Meili Snow Mountain. The monastery is 30 minutes before Benzilan, the halfway rest town for the Shangri-La–Deqin drive.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-sky-50 text-sky-600 text-lg font-bold">塔</span>
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Tower in Tower</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">The Shangri-La Tower in Tower (塔中塔) is the only structure of its kind in China — a smaller golden pagoda completely enclosed within a larger white stupa. Built in 2013 on the site of an ancient Tibetan stupa, it stands in the center of a traffic roundabout near the old town entrance. The inner pagoda contains Buddhist relics, while the outer stupa has prayer wheels around its base that locals spin as they circumambulate.</p>
            <div class="mt-3 flex items-center justify-between rounded-lg bg-white px-3 py-2">
              <span class="text-xs text-slate-400">Entry</span>
              <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">Free</span>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Tip:</strong> Visit at dusk when the golden inner pagoda is illuminated. It is a 10-minute walk from Dukezong's north gate. Join locals in walking clockwise around the base.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-600 text-lg font-bold">街</span>
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Sifang Street Square</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">Sifang Street (四方街) is the central plaza of Dukezong Old Town and the traditional gathering place for Shangri-La's Tibetan community. By day, it is a marketplace with yak jerky vendors and souvenir stalls. After sunset — usually around 7:30 PM — the square transforms. Locals of all ages gather for traditional Tibetan circle dancing (锅庄舞). Everyone is welcome to join — no experience needed, just follow the rhythm.</p>
            <div class="mt-3 flex items-center justify-between rounded-lg bg-white px-3 py-2">
              <span class="text-xs text-slate-400">Best time</span>
              <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">Evening (7:30 PM+)</span>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Tip:</strong> Grab a yak yogurt from a nearby stall, sit on the perimeter steps, and watch the dancing before you join in. The square is ringed by excellent momo (Tibetan dumpling) shops.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

